    "question": {
      ":question/id": "094d20ba-d98f-54df-9146-d20023cb8b00",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x y^{2}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/workspace/evidence/assets/17592186172040/plots/e73758300747786de096b4b21d9ab15e49e5d2eae55025c733c82a2413f7745b.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/workspace/evidence/assets/17592186172040/plots/d2658251fc4b3c22b8e24efc92b771dd8c3b123b22ef285b1b770e3a641180db.png)",
